# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-01-31/pdf/2025-02096.pdf> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/> ,
              <https://www.ne.se/uppslagsverk/encyklopedi/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "1. Candidates (query 1): a list of 99 rivers, written by the compiler to cover every continent and the Nordic countries and including well-known tributaries, was looked up in Wikidata by the titles of their English Wikipedia articles, with every P403 (mouth of the watercourse) statement, its rank and the English and Swedish labels of river and mouth. Rivers whose English article title did not match (the Torne, Ob, Yenisei, Lena, St. Lawrence, Ångermanälven, Tana and Kymijoki) were looked up by their Swedish article titles (query 2). The Lena has no P403 statement and was dropped."@en ,
                 "2. Selection: from the candidates the compiler chose 50 rivers whose mouth is one named water that every source checked agrees on (see Selection). For each, query 4 fetched every P403 statement with its rank, and query 3 every English, Swedish and multilingual (mul) label and alias of the rivers and of the waters they flow into. Query 6 fetched P361 (part of), P403, P200 (inflows) and P31 (instance of) of some of the waters, for the notes; query 7 checked whether any Wikidata item records the Gulf of Ob as the Ob's mouth (none does)."@en ,
                 "3. The back of each card is the value of the river's P403 statement. Where Wikidata gives more than one value (Elbe: North Sea, German Bight, Heligoland Bight; Loire: Bay of Biscay, Loire estuary; Yenisei: Kara Sea, Yenisei Gulf; Volga: Caspian Sea, Kama), the card names the one item that is a sea, ocean, bay or lake in its own right and that the other sources also name, and a note may name a narrower water (Elbe: German Bight; Yenisei: Yenisei Gulf) or the wider one (Loire: Atlantic Ocean); the Volga card has no note. The Volga's second value, the Kama, is its tributary, not its mouth (see quality control)."@en ,
                 "4. Names: the English front is the river's short English name (\"Danube\", \"Amazon\", \"Mississippi\"); \"River\" is kept only where it is part of the name (\"Yellow River\", \"Orange River\"), and the Nordic rivers keep their own names as English does (\"Göta älv\", \"Kemijoki\"). The English back is the Wikidata English label of the water, except \"Gulf of St. Lawrence\" (an English alias, matching the front \"St. Lawrence\"), \"Mississippi\" and \"Murray\" (short forms, matching the fronts). The Swedish front and back are the Wikidata Swedish label, or, where Swedish reference works use another established form, a Swedish alias: Darlingfloden (the title of the Swedish Wikipedia article and NE's headword; the Swedish label is \"Darling\"), Murrayfloden (the form NE uses in its article on the Darling, matching \"Darlingfloden\" on the same card; the Swedish label is \"Murray\") and Bohaihavet (the form in the text of the Swedish Wikipedia article on the Yellow River; the Swedish label is \"Bohai\"). Every Swedish name was compared with the title of the river's Swedish Wikipedia article and with NE's headword or alternative names. NE's headwords for the Yangtze and the Yellow River are \"Chang Jiang\" and \"Huang He\" (NE gives \"Gula floden\" as another name); the cards use the Swedish Wikipedia's and Wikidata's \"Yangtze\" and \"Gula floden\" and give the Chinese names in a note. \"Saint Lawrencefloden\" and \"Saint Lawrenceviken\" follow the Wikidata Swedish labels, the Swedish Wikipedia and NE, not the hyphenated alias \"Saint Lawrence-floden\". The notes' Swedish water names are Wikidata Swedish labels, except \"Jenisejviken\" (Yenisei Gulf), which has no Swedish label or alias in Wikidata and was taken from the Swedish Wikipedia article \"Jenisej\" (infobox and text)."@en ,
                 "5. Wikipedia check: the current English and Swedish Wikipedia article of every river (titles from the Wikidata sitelinks, query 5) was fetched with its revision id through the MediaWiki API (fetch_wiki.py and fetch_wiki2.py), and the infobox fields mouth, mouth_location and mynning and the sentences of the text that name the mouth were extracted (mouths.py, which replaces templates such as unit conversions with \"…\" and removes link markup and references). The card table <scratch>/cards.py holds, for each card, only values that appear in the dossier: the Wikidata items of river and mouth, the names on the card, the notes, the Wikipedia article keys, the NE address and the marker string that picks the quoted sentence. The script make_cards.py (query 14) then wrote the evidence of every card from these extracts and from the saved Wikidata results; the compiler chose for each article the sentence to quote and read every card's evidence against the article."@en ,
                 "6. NE check: the article of each river on Nationalencyklopedin (ne.se) was fetched (fetch_ne.py) and the opening shown to visitors without a subscription was read. Article addresses were guessed from the Swedish names; where that failed (Po, Kongo, Niger) a web search on ne.se found the address form \"kongo-(flod-afrika)\". No NE article was found for the Paraná or the São Francisco, whose cards rest on Wikidata and the two Wikipedias. Britannica was tried first and answered 403 to the research environment: curl -s -A \"solid-memo deck research (https://github.com/antwika/solid-memo)\" -L -o <scratch>/brit-danube.html -w \"%{http_code} %{url_effective} %{size_download}\\n\" https://www.britannica.com/place/Danube-River; it was not used."@en ,
                 "7. Mississippi note: the text of Executive Order 14172 was downloaded from the Government Publishing Office (govinfo.gov) and its Sec. 4 read; the note states only what the order did in January 2025, not the name's later use."@en ,
                 "8. Machine checks: for every card the builder checks against live Wikidata that the river has the P403 value on the back, and that the English and Swedish names on the card are labels or aliases of the river and of the water in that language or in Wikidata's multilingual (mul) set, where they are (\"Torne\" and \"Congo\" are mul labels and \"Mississippi\" a mul alias, not English ones; no English or mul label or alias matches the short English names Mekong, Irrawaddy, Columbia, Mackenzie, Amazon, Paraná, Magdalena, Darling and Murray, which have no label check). make_dossier.py assembled this dossier from cards.json and the text written for it. The notes rest on the Wikidata statements and the order named in each card's evidence. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Selection: 50 rivers: 13 in the rest of Europe (Danube, Rhine, Volga, Don, Elbe, Thames, Seine, Loire, Rhône, Po, Tagus, Tiber, and the Ural, conventionally counted as the Europe–Asia boundary along much of its lower course), 7 Nordic (Göta älv, Klarälven, Torne, Kemijoki, Oulujoki, Vuoksi, Kymijoki), 9 in Asia, 8 in Africa, 7 in North America, 5 in South America and 1 in Australia, chosen by the compiler among the world's best-known rivers, with a few well-known tributaries (Blue and White Nile, Missouri, Darling) and rivers flowing into lakes (Klarälven, Vuoksi, Niagara, Jordan). The mouth is a water named by the river's P403 statement in Wikidata (the Loire into the Bay of Biscay, the Torne into the Bothnian Bay, not the Atlantic or the Gulf of Bothnia). Where Wikidata names several waters (the Elbe, Loire and Yenisei), the card gives the sea or bay that the other sources also name (the North Sea, the Bay of Biscay, the Kara Sea) and the note names the narrower or wider water; a river that joins another flows into that river. Left out because sources name different waters as the mouth or the river ends in several: the Ob (Wikidata: Kara Sea; Wikipedia and NE: the Gulf of Ob), Dalälven (Wikidata: Gävle Bay; Swedish Wikipedia: the Bothnian Sea; English Wikipedia: the Gulf of Bothnia), the Glomma (Wikidata and the Wikipedia infoboxes: Skagerrak; the texts: the Oslofjord), the Vistula (a delta into Gdańsk Bay and the Vistula Lagoon), the Oder (Wikidata: Baltic Sea and Szczecin Lagoon), the Dnieper and Dniester (estuaries ranked above or instead of the Black Sea) and the Kama (Wikidata: the Volga, the Kuybyshev Reservoir and the Chusovaya). Left out because Wikidata names no mouth: the Lena. Left out because their Wikidata mouth is a little-known local water (Lule älv, Ume älv, Indalsälven, Kalix älv, Motala ström, the Neva, the Northern Dvina, the Hudson, the Pechora). Left out to keep the deck at about 50 and avoid repeating the same back: the Amur, Brahmaputra, Kolyma, Yalu, Tigris, Amu Darya, Syr Darya, Murray, Colorado, Rio Grande, Ohio, Uruguay, Senegal, Douro, Guadalquivir, Weser, Main, Moselle, Inn, Daugava, Pite älv and others among the 99 candidates. Several big rivers end in a delta (Danube, Nile, Mekong, Ganges, Niger), but into one water, and are included. The Rhine's distributary IJssel flows into the IJsselmeer, but every source checked names the North Sea as the Rhine's mouth."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The content sources are Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0, and Executive Order 14172, a work of the US Government and not under copyright: the mouths, the English and Swedish names and the facts in the notes come from them, so the deck is CC0 1.0 like the rest of the library. The choice of 50 rivers is the compiler's own, not taken from any list. The English and Swedish Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) and Nationalencyklopedin (all rights reserved) were used for verification only: they confirmed the mouths and the Swedish names that Wikidata gave, and no wording, list or selection was copied from them. The Swedish forms Darlingfloden, Murrayfloden and Bohaihavet, compared with Wikipedia and NE, are Swedish aliases in Wikidata. One Swedish place name in a note, \"Jenisejviken\", is not in Wikidata and was taken from the Swedish Wikipedia; a single name is a fact, not a copyrightable expression, so it does not bring in the CC BY-SA licence. The fronts, backs and notes are the compiler's own wording of facts."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every card's mouth against Wikidata (P403), the English and Swedish Wikipedia and Nationalencyklopedin; the Swedish names against Wikidata sv labels, the Swedish Wikipedia and NE; the build's live Wikidata checks."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 50 cards, the 99 candidate rivers, the sources and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: For all 50 rivers Wikidata's P403, the English and Swedish Wikipedia and (where it says) NE name the same water, or a narrower or wider name for it (Torne: Bothnian Bay or Gulf of Bothnia; Loire: Bay of Biscay or Atlantic; Mackenzie: Beaufort Sea or Arctic Ocean). Two Swedish Wikipedia infoboxes are wrong and their texts right. No Wikidata check was skipped. Candidates with a disputed or split mouth were left out."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 50 cards against live Wikidata (P403, labels, aliases, mul) and general geography; counts and the description."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No card's mouth was found wrong and every check passes. The description's \"as precisely as Wikidata names it\" did not match the Elbe, Yenisei and Loire cards; the Swedish \"Murray\" broke the deck's naming style; \"Don\" and \"Jordan\" got disambiguating front notes; method and evidence called mul labels and aliases English."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 50 cards and the deck metadata: Swedish names and idiom, English wording, language tags, keywords."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Tags, keywords and Swedish names were sound. Fixed: the Darling card's mixed Swedish naming, the word order of the Mississippi note, the dangling subject of the Euphrates note, the Swedish description's P403 gloss and the English documentation's Swedish forms of Finnish and Torne names. The unhyphenated Saint Lawrence compounds are kept and now documented."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every source's licence and role, the attribution in the built TTL, the evidence of all 50 cards (22 spot-checked by fetching), the recorded queries and scripts."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Licensing found compliant (CC0 from Wikidata and a US Government work). Fixed: the Wikipedia and Wikidata source URLs pointed at a list page and a property page; the Swedish \"Jenisejviken\" was undocumented as coming from the Swedish Wikipedia; Murrayfloden; labels.py and cards.py undocumented; NE's usedFor overstated the mouths it confirmed."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 9 cards changed in rounds 1–3 and every third card (22 cards), against live Wikidata, the cited Wikipedia revisions, NE and the EO 14172 PDF; the TTL and MD for every logged fix; the description, method and selection."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every check passes and every fix from rounds 1–3 is in place. Fixed: the description and method step 3 said a note names the narrower water wherever Wikidata gives several mouths, which the Loire and Volga cards do not follow; a double full stop in method step 8."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 50 cards (every P403 and label check re-run against live Wikidata, en/sv/mul), the description, keywords, study direction, selection and method, and every fix logged in rounds 1–4."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; every fix from rounds 1–4 is in place. Two optional suggestions, both applied: the Elbe note now names its subject, and the selection says the Ural lies on the conventional Europe–Asia boundary."@en .

